00:05:08.873 --> 00:05:10.790
And at the current stage
of Add a Cat to That,

00:05:10.790 --> 00:05:15.230
we are using the beta cloud
run, which we made available

00:05:15.230 --> 00:05:16.532
a few weeks back.

00:05:16.532 --> 00:05:17.990
And it's a really
great fit for us.

00:05:17.990 --> 00:05:21.320
We uploaded the image,
used Cloud Build,

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:25.140
and it was pretty much turnkey,
pointed it at our domain.

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:28.140
And we were ready to start
pitching our application

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:29.390
for the next round of funding.

00:05:32.598 --> 00:05:34.390
But this is when the
next question came up,

00:05:34.390 --> 00:05:40.300
which, if we can go
back to the slides,

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:41.930
this is when the
question of security

00:05:41.930 --> 00:05:44.070
finally started to come on.

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:45.600
We're going around.

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:47.250
We're talking to
potential investors,

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:50.790
and we're getting this
question over and over again.

00:05:50.790 --> 00:05:53.400
Everyone wants to know if
our application is secure.

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:54.900
MAX SALTONSTALL: Is it secure?

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:56.692
JEN TONG: That's a
little more complicated.

00:05:56.692 --> 00:06:00.400
I guess we'll be getting into
that with most of the session.

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:02.680
Over and over again,
we heard horror stories

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:08.220
of people's previous investments
ending up in the headlines.

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:10.970
But the truth is security is
not really a binary thing.

00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:14.542
It's not a yes or no question.

00:06:14.542 --> 00:06:17.000
You don't just flip a switch,
and suddenly your application

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:17.910
is secure.

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:20.270
It's much more
complex than that.

00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:22.580
A little fuzzy, maybe even
like a kind of a gradient

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:26.060
is another way of
thinking about it.

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:28.130
To work towards a more
secure application,

00:06:28.130 --> 00:06:30.920
to move further
along that spectrum,

00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:32.990
we need to do some work.

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:35.840
We need to put on
our attacker hat

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:39.690
and do an exercise
called threat modeling.

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:42.245
In threat modeling, what
you do is you take advantage

00:06:42.245 --> 00:06:43.620
of the fact that
you are probably

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:45.260
the best person to
attack your software

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:46.760
since you know
everything about it--

00:06:46.760 --> 00:06:48.520
or most of the things.

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:50.910
And then you use what you
learned from that process

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:52.520
to make your application safer.

00:06:55.390 --> 00:06:57.490
One of the foundations
of this technique

00:06:57.490 --> 00:07:01.012
is that a hacker's probably
not just going to randomly hack

00:07:01.012 --> 00:07:01.720
at your software.

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:04.010
Well, they might,
but probably not.

00:07:04.010 --> 00:07:06.130
They're probably after
something specific.

00:07:06.130 --> 00:07:08.770
They probably have
a goal in mind.

00:07:08.770 --> 00:07:11.660
This might be your user data.

00:07:11.660 --> 00:07:13.990
This might be
financial information

00:07:13.990 --> 00:07:15.640
that you have in a database.

00:07:15.640 --> 00:07:17.350
Or the truth is the
goal could actually

00:07:17.350 --> 00:07:18.670
be outside your organization.

00:07:18.670 --> 00:07:21.070
They might be using
you as a stepping stone

00:07:21.070 --> 00:07:22.270
to get at something else.

00:07:22.270 --> 00:07:23.895
Like, they might want
to take advantage

00:07:23.895 --> 00:07:26.860
of an open source library you
maintain that other people use.

00:07:29.650 --> 00:07:31.900
But they won't be able to
get straight at that goal.

00:07:31.900 --> 00:07:33.358
Because it's
important to you, it's

00:07:33.358 --> 00:07:35.440
probably pretty well protected.

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:38.080
So an attacker is going to look
for intermediate resources.

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:41.710
They're going to
investigate your environment

00:07:41.710 --> 00:07:43.570
and try and find
different things that they

00:07:43.570 --> 00:07:47.320
can take advantage of to pivot
and move around and make it

00:07:47.320 --> 00:07:48.490
further into your system.

00:07:48.490 --> 00:07:49.907
These intermediate
resources could

00:07:49.907 --> 00:07:52.460
be things like servers
running in a cluster,

00:07:52.460 --> 00:07:55.540
but they can also be things
like libraries you depend on

00:07:55.540 --> 00:07:59.658
or even your
developers themselves.

00:07:59.658 --> 00:08:01.200
And as they investigate,
they're also

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:03.700
going to try and learn about
any defensive measures you have

00:08:03.700 --> 00:08:09.570
in place, anything from password
policies, to auditing you do,

00:08:09.570 --> 00:08:11.090
to firewall rules, and the like.

00:08:13.630 --> 00:08:16.180
They're probably going to start
the process of enumerating

00:08:16.180 --> 00:08:17.710
all these things
from information

00:08:17.710 --> 00:08:19.810
they can publicly find
online, like photos

00:08:19.810 --> 00:08:21.220
from your last
company party that

00:08:21.220 --> 00:08:24.120
made it up to LinkedIn somehow.

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:26.460
And maybe they'll get a
foothold in at some point.

00:08:26.460 --> 00:08:29.850
And they'll spend time very
carefully and gradually

00:08:29.850 --> 00:08:32.680
enumerating all those
intermediate resources

00:08:32.680 --> 00:08:34.805
and all those defensive
measures you have in place.

00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:41.480
Until they find a way to string
them all together to wiggle

00:08:41.480 --> 00:08:44.120
around defensive measures
you have in place,

00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:47.180
and strike at that goal, and
get access to that information,

00:08:47.180 --> 00:08:50.300
or plant that malware, or
whatever they want to do.

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:52.010
And this is called a kill chain.

00:08:52.010 --> 00:08:53.825
This part can
happen very quickly.

00:08:57.308 --> 00:08:59.100
But what we want to do
is we want to defend

00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:00.660
against those threats.

00:09:00.660 --> 00:09:03.385
And our first impulse might
be after doing some threat

00:09:03.385 --> 00:09:05.760
modeling and figuring out a
kill chain that someone might

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:08.490
be able to do would be to take
one of our defensive measures

00:09:08.490 --> 00:09:09.690
and just beef it up--

00:09:09.690 --> 00:09:13.440
more firewall rules,
more broken down network,

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:15.550
that kind of stuff.

00:09:15.550 --> 00:09:18.942
But that's not always the
best investment of your time.

00:09:18.942 --> 00:09:20.400
Going straight to
our pet solutions

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:23.380
is not always the
best thing we can do.

00:09:23.380 --> 00:09:26.410
It turns out that often
a better use of your time

00:09:26.410 --> 00:09:29.890
is to focus on removing
unnecessary things that

00:09:29.890 --> 00:09:33.000
could potentially be
intermediate resources,

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:34.710
and of the ones that
you need to keep,

00:09:34.710 --> 00:09:36.805
reducing the attack surface
to make them smaller.

00:09:39.660 --> 00:09:43.270
And in addition to
that, rather than have

00:09:43.270 --> 00:09:47.140
one strong defensive measure
in place in a certain area,

00:09:47.140 --> 00:09:50.620
it's more effective to have
a greater number of easier

00:09:50.620 --> 00:09:52.990
to maintain defensive
measures that overlap,

00:09:52.990 --> 00:09:57.420
so that if one fails, another
one might get triggered.

00:09:57.420 --> 00:10:00.555
And this technique is
called defense in depth.

00:10:00.555 --> 00:10:02.880
So that if one thing
fails for whatever reason,

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:06.500
you have another backup
behind it to help protect you.

00:10:06.500 --> 00:10:08.970
And this is the philosophy that
we believe in very strongly

00:10:08.970 --> 00:10:09.470
at Google.

00:10:12.780 --> 00:10:15.170
So we all have multiple
different things

00:10:15.170 --> 00:10:17.210
we want to protect.

00:10:17.210 --> 00:10:20.240
Today we're going to be talking
about a few different things

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:24.250
that you can do to establish
that defense in depth.

00:10:24.250 --> 00:10:28.790
We're going be talking about
secure supply chain, which

00:10:28.790 --> 00:10:32.810
is about making sure you don't
deploy bad code that someone

00:10:32.810 --> 00:10:36.852
else wrote, or deploy
dependencies that

00:10:36.852 --> 00:10:38.810
might have problems that
we already know about.

00:10:41.448 --> 00:10:43.490
We're going to talk about
automatic vulnerability

00:10:43.490 --> 00:10:46.735
scanning, so that we can
find that low hanging fruit,

00:10:46.735 --> 00:10:48.860
those silly mistakes we
make that we kick ourselves

00:10:48.860 --> 00:10:53.990
when we find, before someone
else finds them for us.

00:10:53.990 --> 00:10:58.020
And we're going to talk about
the nut behind the wheel,

00:10:58.020 --> 00:11:01.590
another potential weakness,
making sure that phishing

00:11:01.590 --> 00:11:02.940
does not cause us a problem.

00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:05.340
Because phishing is something
that we often joke about

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:06.540
and we tease people.

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:08.700
But it's a surprisingly
effective technique

00:11:08.700 --> 00:11:11.220
in offensive security,
despite the fact

00:11:11.220 --> 00:11:14.910
that we were all immune
to phishing, obviously.

00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:18.030
Surprisingly not.

00:11:18.030 --> 00:11:20.790
So first, let's dig into
one of those deeper.

00:11:20.790 --> 00:11:22.760
Let's talk about
secure supply chain

00:11:22.760 --> 00:11:24.530
in the context of
Add a Cat to That.

00:11:27.260 --> 00:11:29.600
So Add a Cat to That is
a very short program.

00:11:29.600 --> 00:11:31.710
Like you saw, it has a
dozen lines of code that's

00:11:31.710 --> 00:11:34.400
kind of the core functionality.

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:38.333
But believe it or not, it
has hundreds of dependencies.

00:11:38.333 --> 00:11:39.750
And one of the
things that made it

00:11:39.750 --> 00:11:41.460
so easy to write
Add a Cat to That

00:11:41.460 --> 00:11:46.190
is we all kind of stand on
the shoulders of giants.

00:11:46.190 --> 00:11:48.410
We get to include a lot
of other people's work

00:11:48.410 --> 00:11:51.170
to get started very quickly
and make very rapid progress

00:11:51.170 --> 00:11:53.530
as developers.

00:11:53.530 --> 00:11:57.250
But some of those things we
depend on might be neglected,

00:11:57.250 --> 00:12:00.380
or someone else might
have made a mistake.

00:12:00.380 --> 00:12:02.770
So that's a really
large surface for things

00:12:02.770 --> 00:12:05.093
to potentially go wrong.

00:12:05.093 --> 00:12:06.260
And we don't want to be sad.

00:12:08.815 --> 00:12:10.440
And in fact, we can
kind of get an idea

00:12:10.440 --> 00:12:13.260
of how big this risk is.

00:12:13.260 --> 00:12:18.270
In 2018, there were over 16,000
published vulnerabilities

00:12:18.270 --> 00:12:22.570
called CVEs in
vulnerability databases.

00:12:22.570 --> 00:12:25.160
And that's way too many
to keep track of manually.

00:12:25.160 --> 00:12:27.010
So if we tried to
subscribe to newsletters

00:12:27.010 --> 00:12:29.170
and then kind of investigate
Add a Cat to That

00:12:29.170 --> 00:12:30.880
each time to see
if we're impacted,

00:12:30.880 --> 00:12:34.230
we're never going to
be able to keep up.

00:12:34.230 --> 00:12:35.855
But we're software
developers, and when

00:12:35.855 --> 00:12:37.940
we encounter a
problem like that,

00:12:37.940 --> 00:12:41.260
we solve it with software.

00:12:41.260 --> 00:12:45.850
And fortunately, we are using
Google Container Registry

00:12:45.850 --> 00:12:48.430
to store our container
images before we push them

00:12:48.430 --> 00:12:50.170
to production in cloud run.

00:12:50.170 --> 00:12:52.165
And I'd like to show
you a feature of that

00:12:52.165 --> 00:12:56.530
that can help us keep
our supply chain secure.

00:12:56.530 --> 00:12:58.300
So with that, let's
go back to the demo.

00:13:01.910 --> 00:13:05.032
So here is the Container
Registry, where all of the Add

00:13:05.032 --> 00:13:09.570
a Cat to That images
are published.

00:13:09.570 --> 00:13:12.230
And if we go over to the
Settings pane of Container

00:13:12.230 --> 00:13:14.390
Registry, we will
see a feature that

00:13:14.390 --> 00:13:17.120
is normally off by default.
It is a beta feature,

00:13:17.120 --> 00:13:18.120
but it is awesome.

00:13:18.120 --> 00:13:19.620
It is worth checking,
even if you're

00:13:19.620 --> 00:13:21.750
a little bit beta skittish.

00:13:21.750 --> 00:13:23.840
We've already enabled
it ahead of time.

00:13:23.840 --> 00:13:26.540
It's called
Vulnerability Scanning.

00:13:26.540 --> 00:13:29.630
And now if we go back
to our list of images

00:13:29.630 --> 00:13:31.750
and go into our web
application image,

00:13:31.750 --> 00:13:36.083
you will see a new column
has appeared on the far side.

00:13:36.083 --> 00:13:37.500
And if we click
into that, we will

00:13:37.500 --> 00:13:41.520
see that our image, which will
only have a few dependencies,

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:44.910
or so we thought, and a
dozen lines of actual code,

00:13:44.910 --> 00:13:49.200
has over 600 vulnerabilities
and only 38 fixes.

00:13:49.200 --> 00:13:53.590
That's like 6% or 7%
that even can be fixed.

00:13:53.590 --> 00:13:57.540
So it's at this point that a lot
of people might start to panic.

00:13:57.540 --> 00:13:58.710
But don't panic.

00:13:58.710 --> 00:13:59.970
It's OK.

00:13:59.970 --> 00:14:02.685
It's better that we know
about these early on, right?

00:14:02.685 --> 00:14:04.060
MAX SALTONSTALL:
How are we going

00:14:04.060 --> 00:14:05.668
to solve 600 vulnerabilities?

00:14:05.668 --> 00:14:07.960
JEN TONG: Well, first, let's
investigate if any of them

00:14:07.960 --> 00:14:10.510
are actually relevant to us
because these vulnerabilities

00:14:10.510 --> 00:14:13.060
are related to the
dependencies we use,

00:14:13.060 --> 00:14:14.500
but they might not
actually impact

00:14:14.500 --> 00:14:16.118
the way we use our application.

00:14:16.118 --> 00:14:18.160
So let's click in on one
of the ImageMagick wands

00:14:18.160 --> 00:14:19.990
because that's one
of the things we use.

00:14:19.990 --> 00:14:22.870
And here, you'll find details
on exactly what it is.

00:14:22.870 --> 00:14:24.970
Like, this one is an
initialized variable

00:14:24.970 --> 00:14:26.483
leading to memory corruption.

00:14:26.483 --> 00:14:28.900
So if we wanted to, we could
dig through the potential use

00:14:28.900 --> 00:14:29.500
cases.

00:14:29.500 --> 00:14:31.180
We could do a threat modeling.

00:14:31.180 --> 00:14:32.290
We could look through
all the use cases

00:14:32.290 --> 00:14:33.748
that Add a Cat to
That does and see

00:14:33.748 --> 00:14:36.295
if this is even relevant to us.

00:14:36.295 --> 00:14:38.670
And even if there's not a fix
we might be able to set up,

00:14:38.670 --> 00:14:39.830
we might be able to
do defense in depth,

00:14:39.830 --> 00:14:41.788
instead of a secondary
measure that protects us

00:14:41.788 --> 00:14:44.090
from this particular issue,
although this one looks

00:14:44.090 --> 00:14:45.325
kind of bad.

00:14:45.325 --> 00:14:47.450
So let's see if we can come
up with a different way

00:14:47.450 --> 00:14:48.640
to mitigate this issue.

00:14:51.460 --> 00:14:54.580
So for that, let's look
at an experiment round.

00:14:54.580 --> 00:15:00.422
So if we go back to the code,
we did an experiment-- well,

00:15:00.422 --> 00:15:01.880
first of all, when
we looked at all

00:15:01.880 --> 00:15:03.170
of our potential
vulnerabilities,

00:15:03.170 --> 00:15:04.712
we noticed that a
whole bunch of them

00:15:04.712 --> 00:15:05.870
are related to ImageMagick.

00:15:05.870 --> 00:15:06.912
ImageMagick is wonderful.

00:15:06.912 --> 00:15:10.310
It is super fast, but it
is also very complicated

00:15:10.310 --> 00:15:12.380
and has a lot of
vulnerabilities that get

00:15:12.380 --> 00:15:13.915
discovered in it as a result.

00:15:13.915 --> 00:15:15.818
So we discover that
there is a pure Python

00:15:15.818 --> 00:15:17.360
implementation we
can do to implement

00:15:17.360 --> 00:15:19.770
the exact same functionality.

00:15:19.770 --> 00:15:21.982
It's a Python library
called Pillow.

00:15:21.982 --> 00:15:23.690
And with just a few
lines of code change,

00:15:23.690 --> 00:15:25.700
we were able to re-implement
Add a Cat to That

00:15:25.700 --> 00:15:29.360
and remove the forked
ImageMagick process from it,

00:15:29.360 --> 00:15:31.772
which is a huge win.

00:15:31.772 --> 00:15:33.230
Another thing we
did was we tweaked

00:15:33.230 --> 00:15:35.300
our Docker file a little bit.

00:15:35.300 --> 00:15:38.060
As much as the Hello
World Python 3.7

00:15:38.060 --> 00:15:40.322
image got us started
fast, it turns out

00:15:40.322 --> 00:15:41.780
it had a whole
bunch of stuff in it

00:15:41.780 --> 00:15:44.450
that we didn't need at all,
which were well represented

00:15:44.450 --> 00:15:46.280
in that vulnerability list.

00:15:46.280 --> 00:15:49.860
So we went and grabbed a
slimmed down base image.

00:15:49.860 --> 00:15:51.800
And there's a lot
of these available.

00:15:51.800 --> 00:15:54.828
We chose Alpine
Linux for this one.

00:15:54.828 --> 00:15:56.370
And we added a little
more work here.

00:15:56.370 --> 00:16:00.900
We had to explicitly install
all of our real dependencies.

00:16:00.900 --> 00:16:03.550
But other than that, the Docker
file's pretty much the same.

00:16:03.550 --> 00:16:05.190
But with that one
little change, boop,

00:16:05.190 --> 00:16:08.220
we shot it back up
to Container Registry

00:16:08.220 --> 00:16:09.880
and stuck it in
the experimental.

00:16:09.880 --> 00:16:12.918
And you will notice that if we
go over to the Vulnerabilities

00:16:12.918 --> 00:16:14.460
tab-- oh, you can
see right it there,

00:16:14.460 --> 00:16:16.410
too-- it shows up in the
interface a few places.

00:16:16.410 --> 00:16:17.850
Surprisingly, I thought
there would still

00:16:17.850 --> 00:16:20.350
be some vulnerabilities, but
it got it all the way back down

00:16:20.350 --> 00:16:23.140
to zero with just a
tiny little change.

00:16:23.140 --> 00:16:26.910
And the truth is that this
change did have a cost.

00:16:26.910 --> 00:16:29.670
It is slower, and it
uses more memory now.

00:16:29.670 --> 00:16:31.830
But that is the decision
that we have to make.

00:16:31.830 --> 00:16:34.530
We have to decide if the
performance overhead is

00:16:34.530 --> 00:16:37.500
worth it to this, or if we
want to put in other maybe

00:16:37.500 --> 00:16:41.400
more complicated or time--

00:16:41.400 --> 00:16:43.770
more difficult interventions
in place to prevent it.

00:16:43.770 --> 00:16:46.020
In this case, at least for
us, we're at the MVP stage.

00:16:46.020 --> 00:16:47.520
We just want to
show some cool demos

00:16:47.520 --> 00:16:49.000
and not get pwned
in the process.

00:16:49.000 --> 00:16:52.150
This is a great solution for us.

00:16:52.150 --> 00:16:54.840
So with that, I guess
we're probably ready to go.

00:16:54.840 --> 00:16:56.340
MAX SALTONSTALL: I
think we're done.

00:16:56.340 --> 00:16:56.925
Yeah?

00:16:56.925 --> 00:16:58.410
JEN TONG: Well, I don't know.

00:16:58.410 --> 00:17:00.077
Why don't you tell
us a little bit more?

00:17:00.077 --> 00:17:01.952
MAX SALTONSTALL: Let's
go back to the slides.

00:17:01.952 --> 00:17:03.900
So I think everything's
secure now, right?

00:17:03.900 --> 00:17:06.119
Because we fixed all
of the dependency

00:17:06.119 --> 00:17:09.214
chain, and we just eliminated
600 vulnerabilities

00:17:09.214 --> 00:17:11.339
in a matter of minutes by
making those two changes.

00:17:11.339 --> 00:17:13.420
JEN TONG: And we review
all our code every night.

00:17:13.420 --> 00:17:15.420
MAX SALTONSTALL: Especially
2:00 in the morning.

00:17:15.420 --> 00:17:17.040
JEN TONG: Usually at 2:00
to 3:00 in the morning.

00:17:17.040 --> 00:17:18.460
MAX SALTONSTALL: The
night before the demo.

00:17:18.460 --> 00:17:19.589
JEN TONG: Yeah, after.

00:17:19.589 --> 00:17:21.010
MAX SALTONSTALL: So maybe
we've made some mistakes.

00:17:21.010 --> 00:17:22.593
Maybe you all have
made some mistakes,

00:17:22.593 --> 00:17:24.630
and your code is
not 100% perfect.

00:17:24.630 --> 00:17:27.112
Because we all
introduce some bugs.

00:17:27.112 --> 00:17:29.070
Well, I also want to tell
you about a tool that

00:17:29.070 --> 00:17:30.240
can help you there.

00:17:30.240 --> 00:17:34.230
So Google's web security scanner
will do automated testing

00:17:34.230 --> 00:17:37.020
of your deployed code
to find those bugs

00:17:37.020 --> 00:17:38.760
that you've introduced
vulnerabilities

00:17:38.760 --> 00:17:39.750
unintentionally.

00:17:39.750 --> 00:17:40.800
And rather than tell
you all about it.

00:17:40.800 --> 00:17:41.633
Let's just show you.

00:17:41.633 --> 00:17:42.778
Let's switch to the laptop.

00:17:42.778 --> 00:17:43.403
JEN TONG: Sure.

00:17:45.618 --> 00:17:47.410
MAX SALTONSTALL: So
we're going to bring up

00:17:47.410 --> 00:17:53.030
Cloud Security Scanner
once we switch to our demo.

00:17:53.030 --> 00:17:58.052
And what you can see here
is the simple web UI in GCP.

00:17:58.052 --> 00:18:00.260
Let's make a new scan just
to walk through the steps.

00:18:00.260 --> 00:18:01.778
It's very, very fast.

00:18:01.778 --> 00:18:03.320
So as we make a new
scan, we're going

00:18:03.320 --> 00:18:06.620
to point this at a
staging URL for our app

00:18:06.620 --> 00:18:09.560
because this is actually going
to be fuzz testing our web app.

00:18:09.560 --> 00:18:12.560
And you don't want to point that
at your production instance.

00:18:12.560 --> 00:18:13.977
But you're going
to be interesting

00:18:13.977 --> 00:18:15.590
a whole lot of
wacky data, and it

00:18:15.590 --> 00:18:17.900
might corrupt your application.

00:18:17.900 --> 00:18:21.050
So point it at something
in staging your dev.

00:18:21.050 --> 00:18:23.467
We've put in the Kubernetes
pod address there.

00:18:23.467 --> 00:18:25.550
The rest of these things
you can leave as default.

00:18:25.550 --> 00:18:27.300
I would head down and
make this run daily,

00:18:27.300 --> 00:18:29.030
so it can automatically
update you

00:18:29.030 --> 00:18:32.470
when any new issues are
found because of a new push.

00:18:32.470 --> 00:18:34.970
So we're going to say this is
going to take about 15 minutes

00:18:34.970 --> 00:18:37.910
or so to actually get going,
so let me show you one cat

00:18:37.910 --> 00:18:40.430
scan that we made earlier,
just so you can see what

00:18:40.430 --> 00:18:41.840
some of that output looks like.

00:18:41.840 --> 00:18:44.870
Now our code's
totally safe, right?

00:18:44.870 --> 00:18:45.470
Right?

00:18:45.470 --> 00:18:46.930
JEN TONG: There's some
issues, it looks like.

00:18:46.930 --> 00:18:47.990
Scanner found stuff.

00:18:47.990 --> 00:18:48.300
MAX SALTONSTALL: All right.

00:18:48.300 --> 00:18:50.425
Let's see what the cross-site
scripting issues are.

00:18:50.425 --> 00:18:51.800
JEN TONG: OK,
those can be scary.

00:18:51.800 --> 00:18:53.883
MAX SALTONSTALL: Yeah, so
the web security scanner

00:18:53.883 --> 00:18:55.970
starts crawling around
all the URLs it can find

00:18:55.970 --> 00:18:58.250
and starts looking for
these known vulnerabilities.

00:18:58.250 --> 00:19:00.950
In this case, it found a
cross-site scripting bug

00:19:00.950 --> 00:19:03.540
in our admin login page.

00:19:03.540 --> 00:19:05.060
And what it's
giving us right here

00:19:05.060 --> 00:19:08.210
is the exact way to
replicate that bug,

00:19:08.210 --> 00:19:10.860
so that we can troubleshoot
and then fix it.

00:19:10.860 --> 00:19:12.295
So we're going to make--

00:19:12.295 --> 00:19:12.920
JEN TONG: Sure.

00:19:12.920 --> 00:19:15.240
Let's take a look at
the test data it gave us

00:19:15.240 --> 00:19:16.260
so we can reproduce it.

00:19:16.260 --> 00:19:18.633
I'm going to drop
that into a new file.

00:19:18.633 --> 00:19:21.050
MAX SALTONSTALL: So we're going
to show you this bug live,

00:19:21.050 --> 00:19:23.360
and then we're going
to fix it right now, so

00:19:23.360 --> 00:19:26.240
that we can knock yet
another vulnerability off

00:19:26.240 --> 00:19:27.470
of Add a Cat to That.

00:19:27.470 --> 00:19:30.840
Because we don't want
our cats to be insecure.

00:19:30.840 --> 00:19:32.880
So taking that HTML from
Web Security Scanner,

00:19:32.880 --> 00:19:35.685
we can see here that
doesn't look right.

00:19:35.685 --> 00:19:37.560
JEN TONG: No, it should
say the email address

00:19:37.560 --> 00:19:40.290
of the person who tried to
log in, not some weird stuff.

00:19:40.290 --> 00:19:41.140
MAX SALTONSTALL: Yeah,
that's pretty weird.

00:19:41.140 --> 00:19:43.650
Let's go to the login page HTML
and see if we can fix this.

00:19:43.650 --> 00:19:45.600
JEN TONG: OK, it's right here.

00:19:45.600 --> 00:19:47.140
MAX SALTONSTALL:
So this login page,

00:19:47.140 --> 00:19:49.980
which is called out by
Web Security Scanner,

00:19:49.980 --> 00:19:52.110
this is where that
HTML came from.

00:19:52.110 --> 00:19:55.860
And yeah, I remember
now what happened.

00:19:55.860 --> 00:19:57.810
Back when we were writing
this the first time,

00:19:57.810 --> 00:19:59.568
we assumed anything
submitted to this form

00:19:59.568 --> 00:20:01.610
was going to be totally
safe because it was going

00:20:01.610 --> 00:20:03.190
to be just coming from inside.

00:20:03.190 --> 00:20:04.260
JEN TONG: Right,
because we wanted people

00:20:04.260 --> 00:20:05.730
to have email addresses
with pictures.

00:20:05.730 --> 00:20:06.810
MAX SALTONSTALL: Yeah, now that
we're getting ready to launch,

00:20:06.810 --> 00:20:08.580
maybe we should reconsider that.

00:20:08.580 --> 00:20:09.330
JEN TONG: That's an easy fix.

00:20:09.330 --> 00:20:10.290
MAX SALTONSTALL:
Let's take that out.

00:20:10.290 --> 00:20:11.508
JEN TONG: Remove that.

00:20:11.508 --> 00:20:13.050
MAX SALTONSTALL:
All right, now we're

00:20:13.050 --> 00:20:15.348
allowing the cross-site
scripting prevention

00:20:15.348 --> 00:20:16.890
that we had actually
disabled before.

00:20:16.890 --> 00:20:18.900
I'm sorry for the
double negative.

00:20:18.900 --> 00:20:21.990
So we push that fixed build
to production right now.

00:20:21.990 --> 00:20:25.020
And the coolest part about
this whole process and the tool

00:20:25.020 --> 00:20:27.090
is that the Web Security
Scanner doesn't cost you

00:20:27.090 --> 00:20:28.140
anything to use.

00:20:28.140 --> 00:20:29.290
This is free.

00:20:29.290 --> 00:20:30.150
It's very easy.

00:20:30.150 --> 00:20:31.370
It's not perfect.

00:20:31.370 --> 00:20:32.870
Neither is your
code, but it's going

00:20:32.870 --> 00:20:36.060
to catch a lot of those
common, easy to prevent errors.

00:20:36.060 --> 00:20:38.760
It's not going to protect you
from a sophisticated attack

00:20:38.760 --> 00:20:40.380
or a new clever zero-day.

00:20:40.380 --> 00:20:43.680
But it will help you with
things that may have slipped

00:20:43.680 --> 00:20:45.717
past your code review process.

00:20:45.717 --> 00:20:47.550
Very low friction, and
it's fire and forget.

00:20:47.550 --> 00:20:49.865
Once you set up that daily
scan, it's going to keep going.

00:20:49.865 --> 00:20:50.380
All right.

00:20:50.380 --> 00:20:53.070
Let's go back to the slides.

00:20:53.070 --> 00:20:55.580
So we have secured
our dependency chain

00:20:55.580 --> 00:20:57.440
by fixing some of
those library issues.

00:20:57.440 --> 00:20:59.790
We've made sure
our code is secure.

00:20:59.790 --> 00:21:01.898
Now we're safe, right?

00:21:01.898 --> 00:21:03.440
There's no possible
way someone could

00:21:03.440 --> 00:21:06.538
compromise Add a Cat to That.

00:21:06.538 --> 00:21:08.080
Well, what if somebody
manages to get

00:21:08.080 --> 00:21:12.040
into my account or Jen's account
with a good phishing attack?

00:21:12.040 --> 00:21:15.930
All of those previous
protections are useless.

00:21:15.930 --> 00:21:17.360
So I want to talk
about the people

00:21:17.360 --> 00:21:19.340
and how you can
secure the people

00:21:19.340 --> 00:21:21.760
with better authentication.

00:21:21.760 --> 00:21:24.460
A typical login flow
looks something like this.

00:21:24.460 --> 00:21:27.298
You present your login and
password to the server.

00:21:27.298 --> 00:21:28.090
And then you're in.

00:21:28.090 --> 00:21:30.100
Oh no.

00:21:30.100 --> 00:21:33.850
Somebody was actually giving
you a very clever phishing page.

00:21:33.850 --> 00:21:35.330
You've given them
your credentials.

00:21:35.330 --> 00:21:37.205
And now they're logging
in and doing anything

00:21:37.205 --> 00:21:39.080
that you could have done.

00:21:39.080 --> 00:21:40.480
They can modify
your source code.

00:21:40.480 --> 00:21:42.040
They could add some malware.

00:21:42.040 --> 00:21:44.800
They could sign a malicious
binary with your keys.

00:21:44.800 --> 00:21:46.570
This is terrible.

00:21:46.570 --> 00:21:49.000
There's a lot of bad things,
but you think, I'm good.

00:21:49.000 --> 00:21:53.097
I have this one time password
that my phone gives me.

00:21:53.097 --> 00:21:54.805
JEN TONG: It doesn't
always work, though.

00:21:54.805 --> 00:21:56.380
MAX SALTONSTALL: Aw, man.

00:21:56.380 --> 00:21:59.200
So the phisher can just take
that one time password, too.

00:21:59.200 --> 00:22:00.730
And now they're
passing it forward,

00:22:00.730 --> 00:22:03.120
and they're logging in as
you just as successfully.

00:22:03.120 --> 00:22:05.170
This doesn't actually
prevent phishing.

00:22:05.170 --> 00:22:07.360
So I want to tell you
about a better way.

00:22:07.360 --> 00:22:09.310
These security keys,
which you can either

00:22:09.310 --> 00:22:13.900
get as USB key or Bluetooth,
and now you can actually

00:22:13.900 --> 00:22:15.880
use your Android phone,
thanks to announcement

00:22:15.880 --> 00:22:17.650
a few weeks ago from Google.

00:22:17.650 --> 00:22:19.990
These will do a much
stronger second factor

00:22:19.990 --> 00:22:21.580
to make your login secure.

00:22:21.580 --> 00:22:24.130
So I want to replay
that previous scenario

00:22:24.130 --> 00:22:27.400
in a way that's going to
stop phishing in its tracks.

00:22:27.400 --> 00:22:30.640
Instead of being complete,
after you give login

00:22:30.640 --> 00:22:34.090
name and password, the server
replies with a challenge.

00:22:34.090 --> 00:22:36.800
It knows that you registered a
security key onto your account

00:22:36.800 --> 00:22:37.300
already.

00:22:37.300 --> 00:22:40.630
And it has the public encryption
key for that security key.

00:22:40.630 --> 00:22:43.780
So you get that challenge
back, and your security key,

00:22:43.780 --> 00:22:46.450
when you tap it, is actually
verifying the domain

00:22:46.450 --> 00:22:47.680
that it's talking to.

00:22:47.680 --> 00:22:50.200
That means a phishing
domain, even a very cleverly

00:22:50.200 --> 00:22:52.750
disguised one with a URL that
looks almost exactly the same,

00:22:52.750 --> 00:22:54.708
except for that weird
ASCII character that it's

00:22:54.708 --> 00:22:56.800
using that's not really
an O, but looks like an O,

00:22:56.800 --> 00:22:57.730
won't work.

00:22:57.730 --> 00:22:59.920
The key won't sign
that challenge

00:22:59.920 --> 00:23:03.400
and return if the domain isn't
exactly what it was set up

00:23:03.400 --> 00:23:04.490
with.

00:23:04.490 --> 00:23:05.860
The domain checks out.

00:23:05.860 --> 00:23:08.590
Your security key signs
it, returns a challenge.

00:23:08.590 --> 00:23:10.960
Now the server can
validate that you

00:23:10.960 --> 00:23:13.210
used a key that it
expected that you already

00:23:13.210 --> 00:23:14.710
registered in the past.

00:23:14.710 --> 00:23:17.100
So it knows that you not
only possess your password,

00:23:17.100 --> 00:23:20.260
but you also possess that key,
something you know something

00:23:20.260 --> 00:23:20.980
you have.

00:23:20.980 --> 00:23:24.130
Now you've authenticated
in a way that's extremely

00:23:24.130 --> 00:23:25.307
resistant to phishing.

00:23:25.307 --> 00:23:26.890
We've been using
this a lot at Google,

00:23:26.890 --> 00:23:27.850
and it's really effective.

00:23:27.850 --> 00:23:28.892
Let's switch to the demo.

00:23:28.892 --> 00:23:30.351
I'm going to show
you how it works.

00:23:30.351 --> 00:23:33.130
So Jen, why don't you log in?

00:23:33.130 --> 00:23:36.050
Here's your account,
put in the password.

00:23:36.050 --> 00:23:39.310
And now it's going to
ask for one more step--

00:23:39.310 --> 00:23:41.290
security key.

00:23:41.290 --> 00:23:45.270
Plug that in, give it a tap.

00:23:45.270 --> 00:23:46.560
All right, we are in.

00:23:46.560 --> 00:23:49.110
Now, Jen's a super
admin on this domain.

00:23:49.110 --> 00:23:51.720
We definitely want to
protect those accounts.

00:23:51.720 --> 00:23:53.798
It's really, really important.

00:23:53.798 --> 00:23:56.340
So I would recommend that you
use security keys for everyone.

00:23:56.340 --> 00:23:58.315
It's not practical
in all circumstances.

00:23:58.315 --> 00:23:59.940
So let's say you want
to set it up just

00:23:59.940 --> 00:24:01.050
for your engineering team.

00:24:01.050 --> 00:24:03.383
Let's go into the security
settings in our Google Admin,

00:24:03.383 --> 00:24:06.060
and we're going to go
look at our basic settings

00:24:06.060 --> 00:24:08.880
and get into the advanced
2-factor 2-step security

00:24:08.880 --> 00:24:10.270
settings.

00:24:10.270 --> 00:24:13.780
From here, we can choose
what kind of enforcement.

00:24:13.780 --> 00:24:17.490
Let's turn on 2-factor
for end of May.

00:24:17.490 --> 00:24:19.925
But actually, we also
want to make sure

00:24:19.925 --> 00:24:22.300
that we're doing this for
people where it really matters.

00:24:22.300 --> 00:24:24.980
So we can set it up just
for the engineering group.

00:24:24.980 --> 00:24:26.520
JEN TONG: Right, we can
probably get them keys in time.

00:24:26.520 --> 00:24:28.440
MAX SALTONSTALL: The whole
two-person engineering team.

00:24:28.440 --> 00:24:29.190
JEN TONG: Exactly.

00:24:29.190 --> 00:24:30.630
MAX SALTONSTALL: Perfect.

00:24:30.630 --> 00:24:33.940
So we can select the
engineering team right here.

00:24:33.940 --> 00:24:37.230
And now we can say turn on
enforcement for the end of May.

00:24:37.230 --> 00:24:40.950
And you can decide what kinds
of 2-step authentication

00:24:40.950 --> 00:24:42.383
you're going to allow.

00:24:42.383 --> 00:24:43.800
I think we only
want security keys

00:24:43.800 --> 00:24:46.600
because as we just
learned, an OTP is just

00:24:46.600 --> 00:24:48.720
as phishable as a password.

00:24:48.720 --> 00:24:50.950
As soon as we save
this, we're set.

00:24:53.700 --> 00:24:54.770
It's as easy as that.

00:24:54.770 --> 00:24:55.270
All right.

00:24:55.270 --> 00:24:57.370
Let's go back to the slides.

00:24:57.370 --> 00:25:01.370
So we've had you here
for a little while.

00:25:01.370 --> 00:25:03.370
We've been working on
an Add a Cat to That.

00:25:03.370 --> 00:25:04.120
What do you think?

00:25:04.120 --> 00:25:07.170
Have we made it more secure?

00:25:07.170 --> 00:25:08.490
Yeah?

00:25:08.490 --> 00:25:10.060
Are we done?

00:25:10.060 --> 00:25:11.410
No?

00:25:11.410 --> 00:25:13.350
Well, let's review
what we have done.

00:25:13.350 --> 00:25:17.310
We've secured our supply chain
with Google Container Registry

00:25:17.310 --> 00:25:19.320
and scanned to find
some dependencies

00:25:19.320 --> 00:25:21.330
that we needed to remove.

00:25:21.330 --> 00:25:23.998
We have looked at our
own code in our web app

00:25:23.998 --> 00:25:26.040
to find some cross-site
scripting vulnerabilities

00:25:26.040 --> 00:25:27.415
that we could also fix.

00:25:27.415 --> 00:25:29.040
And we've added
stronger authentication

00:25:29.040 --> 00:25:30.600
for our engineering
team, so it's

00:25:30.600 --> 00:25:32.190
much harder for
someone malicious

00:25:32.190 --> 00:25:35.090
to take over one
of our accounts.

00:25:35.090 --> 00:25:37.323
But you're not all
making Add a Cat to That.

00:25:37.323 --> 00:25:38.990
At least, I hope not
because if you are,

00:25:38.990 --> 00:25:41.900
I have a lawyer who
needs to talk to you.

00:25:41.900 --> 00:25:43.950
Your software is
different than ours.

00:25:43.950 --> 00:25:45.620
And that means the
threats you face

00:25:45.620 --> 00:25:48.488
are going to be different
than what we're looking at.

00:25:48.488 --> 00:25:50.030
And what's important
to you might not

00:25:50.030 --> 00:25:52.460
be as important to us.

00:25:52.460 --> 00:25:55.680
You need to have your
own security strategy.

00:25:55.680 --> 00:25:58.938
So this is the
beginning, and I hope

00:25:58.938 --> 00:26:00.980
that you'll explore some
of the security features

00:26:00.980 --> 00:26:04.160
in Google Cloud or the other
platforms that you're using.

00:26:04.160 --> 00:26:06.710
And I hope that you'll do
a little bit of exploration

00:26:06.710 --> 00:26:07.708
of the documentation.

00:26:07.708 --> 00:26:09.500
I also want you to do
some threat modeling.

00:26:09.500 --> 00:26:11.840
Put on that attacker
hat, figure out

00:26:11.840 --> 00:26:14.300
what someone who knows a
lot about your application

00:26:14.300 --> 00:26:17.300
could do, if they were
feeling especially nasty.

00:26:20.490 --> 00:26:22.710
This is the start
of your journey,

00:26:22.710 --> 00:26:25.052
but it's just the beginning.

00:26:25.052 --> 00:26:26.510
Thank you very much
for joining us.

00:26:26.510 --> 00:26:30.160
[MUSIC PLAYING]

